 "text": "What actually is GitHub, and why does everyone building with AI keep mentioning it? Here's the exact routine. Before you build anything, tell Claude to set up Git and connect the project to GitHub. It creates the repo and saves the first version for you. Then, every time something works, you say commit. That stores a snapshot of your whole project with a note on what changed. Next, say push, and that save lands on GitHub, so your work survives even if your laptop doesn't. When a prompt breaks your app, don't argue with it. Tell Claude to go back to the last commit that worked, and you're there in seconds. Want to test a risky idea? Ask for a branch. Claude builds it on the side, and what already works stays untouched. If you like the result, merge it back into the main version. If you don't, delete the branch and nothing else changes. And when a version is ready to show people, tag it v1, so you can always find it again. That's the whole habit. Commit, push, branch and tag, and the AI types every command. Subscribe to learn more about AI.",
